{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-214041",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\n- Consider the pair $(\\beta ,\\alpha )$ whose second component is $\\alpha .$ It can't be linked with any pair from $R$ since none of them have a first component of $\\alpha $ and a second component other than $\\alpha .$\n\n- Consider the pair $(\\delta ,\\alpha )$ whose second component is $\\alpha .$ It can't be linked with any pair from $R$ since none of them have a first component of $\\alpha $ and a second component other than $\\alpha .$\n\n- Consider the pair $(\\delta ,\\beta )$ whose second component is $\\beta .$ It can be linked with the following pair from $R$ whose first component is $\\beta :$ \n\n- Linking $(\\delta ,\\beta )$ with $(\\beta ,\\alpha )$ requires that $(\\delta ,\\alpha )\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is $\\text{transitive}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
